Sval: a service worker runs only for an origin whose script the policy allows

The script policy (javascript.enabled and its per-site exceptions in the
domain policy, which SvalJavascript keeps) is read when a window global is
made (xpc::Scriptability). Nothing in dom/serviceworkers reads it, so a site
whose JavaScript is off would still have its registered service worker
intercept its navigations and run on push, sync and messages: the origin's
script would run with no page script at all.

Scriptability::AllowedByPolicy answers for a principal as a window global
would: immune (system, expanded, an extension, pdf.js, a scriptable about:
page) or allowed by the policy. ShouldPrepareForIntercept does not hand a
navigation to the worker of an origin it refuses, so the page loads from the
network and is not controlled. SpawnWorkerIfNeeded refuses such a worker for
every event, and stops one already running, so turning a site off reaches a
worker an open tab is keeping alive. A refused fetch falls back to the
network (ResetInterception). The registration and its caches are left alone,
so turning the site back on brings the worker back.

ServiceWorkerUpdateJob::Update fails an update or registration of such an
origin. Its script comparison reads the cache through a sandbox of the
origin's principal, which the policy blocks, and a blocked realm runs none of
its promise jobs (CanRunJSCallback), so the comparison never finished. The
soft update a controlled navigation schedules then held the scope's job
queue, and an unregister of the scope waited behind it for good.

